===Noun===
{{en-noun|duodena|s}}

# {{lb|en|anatomy}} The first part of the [[small intestine]], starting at the lower end of the [[stomach]] and extending to the [[jejunum]].
#* {{RQ:RBrtn AntmyMlncly|chapter=Dißimilar Parts|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=itg_AQAAMAAJ&pg=RA1-PA17|part=1|section=1|member=2|subsection=4|page=17|republished=yes|passage=From the ''Stomacke'' to the very ''Fundament'', are produced the ''Guts'' or ''Inteſtina'', which ſerue a little to alter and diſtribute the ''Chilus'', and convey away the excrements. They are divided into ſmall and great, by reason of their ſite and ſubſtance, ſlender or thicker. The ſlender is '''''Duodenum''''' or whole gut, which is next to the ſtomacke, ſome twelue inches long (ſaith ''Fuſchius''.)}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=A[llan] M[ullen]|authorlink=Allan Mullen|title=An Anatomical Account of the Elephant Accidentally Burnt in Dublin, on Fryday, June 17. in the Year 1681. Sent in a Letter to Sir [[w:William Petty|Will[iam] Petty]], Fellow of the Royal Society. Together with a Relation of New Anatomical Observations in the Eyes of Animals: Communicated in another Letter to the Honourable [[w:Robert Boyle|R[obert] Boyle]], Esq.; Fellow of the Same Society|location=London|publisher=Printed for Sam[uel] Smith, bookseller, at the Prince's Arms in [[w:St Paul's Cathedral|St. Paul's Church-Yard]]|year=1682|page=19|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=qG2d4OonrvMC&pg=PA19|oclc=262025874|passage=The ''Inteſtina tenuia'' were burnt in many places, but not quite through any where, excepting two or three. {{...}} The length of that which is commonly call'd the '''''Duodenum''''', was about 4½ Foot.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Daniel Oliver|chapter=Nutritive Functions|title=First Lines of Physiology; Designed for the Use of Students of Medicine|edition=2nd|location=Philadelphia, Pa.|publisher=Published by Herman Hooker, Corner of Fifth and [[w:Chestnut Street (Philadelphia)|Chestnut Streets]]|year=1840|page=251|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=1d80AQAAMAAJ&pg=PA251|oclc=191247512|passage=The '''duodenum''' receives the chyme from the stomach, and has generally been believed to accomplish the second digestion, or the conversion of chyme into ''chyle''. In the '''duodenum''' it meets with the bile, pancreatic and intestinal fluids, loses its acid properties, and becomes alkaline, probably by the agency of the soda of the bile; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=B[enjamin] Thompson Lowne|chapter=|title=Descriptive Catalogue of the Teratological Series in the Museum of the Royal College of Surgeons of England|location=London|publisher=Printed for the [[w:Royal College of Surgeons of England|College]], and Sold by [[w:Robert Hardwicke|R[obert] Hardwicke]], 192 {{w|Piccadilly}}|year=1872|section=series II (Abnormal Conditions Affecting the Axis in Animals), class II (Duplicity), subseries VI (In Mammals), subclass II (Homologous Union)|page=27, preparation 115|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=0IAaAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA27|oclc=970700900|passage=The '''duodena''' open into a common pouch just below the entrance of the gall-ducts. The intestine was single from the confluence of the '''duodena''' to the termination of the jejunum.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|chapter=Retinaldehyde|editor=Q. Ashton Acton|title=Retinoids—Advances in Research and Application|edition=2013|location=Atlanta, Ga.|publisher={{w|ScholarlyEditions}}|year=2013|page=36|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=H_zYlbxwXbEC&pg=PA36|isbn=978-1-481-69215-1|passage='''Duodenums''' were dissected out, fixed and photographed.}}

